.Dd February 6, 2026
.Dt VFS_MOUNT 9
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm vfs_mount
.Nd mount a filesystem
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/param.h
.In sys/mount.h
.In sys/vnode.h
.Ft int
.Fn vfs_mount "struct mount *mp" "const char *path" "void *data" "struct nameidata *ndp" "struct thread *td"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Fn vfs_mount
function mounts a filesystem into the system's namespace.
It performs the following operations:
.Bl -enum
.It
Look up the mount point vnode using
.Fa ndp .
.It
Verify that the mount point is a directory and is not already a mount point.
.It
Initialize the
.Fa mp
structure.
.It
Call the filesystem-specific VFS_MOUNT operation.
.It
On success, insert
.Fa mp
into the global mount list and link it to the mount point vnode.
.El
.Pp
table arguments:
.Bl -tag -width ".Fa mntflags"
.It Fa mp
Structure representing the filesystem to be mounted.
If NULL, a new structure is allocated.
.It Fa path
Path to the directory where the filesystem will be mounted.
.It Fa data
Filesystem-specific data (arguments).
.It Fa ndp
Nameidata structure used for path lookup.
.It Fa td
The thread performing the mount.
.El
.Sh RETURN VALUES
The
.Fn vfs_mount
function returns 0 on success, or an error code on failure.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width ".Bq Er ENOTDIR"
.It Bq Er ENOTDIR
The path is not a directory.
.It Bq Er EBUSY
The path is already a mount point.
.It Bq Er ENOMEM
Insufficient memory to allocate internal structures.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr vfs_unmount 9 ,
.Xr vfs_start 9 ,
.Xr vfs_root 9
